On 22 Jun., 13:22, Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:
> Derek Ray <moot@just_a_spamtrap_anyway.com> wrote:
> > Janis wrote:
> >> I think the mazes should be changed fundamentally
> >> in a different way and want to bring to mind my
> >> proposal of larger maze corridors.
> > This proposal has not been forgotten, just
> > temporarily held aside.
>
> Larger maze corridors would make mazes at the
> existing dungeon level size much, much more trivial.

Depends on what you find difficult or trivial in the mazes. Solving
Nethack's (ordinary) types of mazes is trivial whatever the area is,
larger or smaller. In other words; "solving" mazes are just a time
factor (thus boredom) and no intellectual challenge whatsoever (IMO).

The proposal serves two goals; reduce the size of "twisted passages",
thus less boredom, and make the maze levels less trivial by allowing
more monsters to surround you, thus demanding somewhat more advanced
problem solving techiques. (Remember, once you reach Gehennoms mazes
people do not die that often; so a side effect - you may consider it
as well as a primary goal, though - is to balance the advanced game.)

> A precursor to such a fix is first to make the x,y
> sizes of the dungeon working parameters (they
> already exist in the source code, IIRC, but I don't
> think most of the code respects them) for
> non-prebuilt levels, and to make the levels larger
> for mazes with wider corridors, so there is some
> challenge remaining to those levels _as mazes_.

There are also some problems with the hybrid levels, half fixed by the
dungeon definition file and the other half generated; an increased
corridor width would not fit smoothly to the borders of the fixed
parts (and will need some slight rewrite) and would also not fit to
the screen borders.

The latter could be avoided by adjusting just the scaling of the x-
coordinate by 2 and leave the y-coordinate scaling untouched. (This
would have the additional side effect that the x/y dimension mismatch
would be alleviated a bit (from approx. 1:3.5 to 1:1.8 ?) leading to
a less streched maze topology.

> It is worth noting that the proposal to make maze
> corridors wider serves only to make the game more
> difficult, not, necessarily, to make maze levels
> inherently interesting.

(More difficult? And more trivial? See above.)

Mazes are not interesting (to me); it's good to have them for the
flair in Nethack (varietas delectat; and I am in favour of randomly
generated dungeon maps) but intellectually they are uninteresting
(that's independent of my proposal).

> Is the wrong problem being solved here?

I don't know what problem you address (or do not address). My proposal
addressed the above presented goals.

